In conclusion, reactions of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and single or mixed linear ligands bearing methyl or azide groups lead to highly stable isoreticular $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs with tunable loadings of azide group inside the pores. For the first time, the system offers an ideal platform for facile pore surface engineering by introducing various functional groups with controlled loadings via click reaction. Remarkably, the resultant click products have been demonstrated to possess well-retained framework and accessible functionalized pores. This work has enabled a general approach for introducing diverse functional groups, especially large and/or reactive groups, into MOF pores that otherwise could not be directly synthesized by conventional solvothermal reactions. The tailored pore surface with the control of both type and density of functional groups in MOFs will extend their functionalities further toward broader applications.
